Record Types {#chapter-types}
=======================================

## Definition ## {#section-types-definition}

Every [=Record=] MUST be assigned to a <dfn>Record Type</dfn>. A Record type is defined by a SchemaRecord ([[#chapter-schemarecord]]). It defines the schema of the Content of a Version, the permitted states and transitions, and the format requirements per state. A Record type MAY be a subclass of exactly one other Record type.


## Core Record Types ## {#section-record-types-core}

RWP defines the following core [=Record types=]:

**Information Records:**
An <dfn>InformationRecord</dfn> is a Record whose primary purpose is to define or document a specific information object.
- `ObjectRecord` (TBD)
- `DocumentRecord` (TBD)

**Relation Records:**
A <dfn>RelationRecord</dfn> is a Record whose primary purpose is to establish, preserve, and make provable a meaningful relationship between Records.
- `CaseRecord` (see [CaseRecord Specification](#chapter-caserecord))
- `AssertionRecord` (see [AssertionRecord  Specification](#chapter-assertionrecord))

**Agent Records:**
An [=AgentRecord=] is a Record whose primary purpose is to identify an entity that bears responsibility for a [=Record=], (see [AgendRecord Specification](#chapter-agentrecord))
- Various (see [AgentRecord Specification](#chapter-agentrecord))

**System Records:**
A <dfn>SystemRecord</dfn> is a Record whose primary purpose is to define, document, or prove a RecordWeb system rule, operation, or capability.
- `SchemaRecord` (see [SchemaRecord](#chapter-schemarecord))
- `DeletionRecord` (see [Payload Deletion](#chapter-deletionrecord))
- `MergeRecord` (TBD)
- `MigrationRecord` (TBD)
- `ConformanceRecord` (see [Conformance and Implementation Claims](#chapter-conformancerecord))

The conformance requirements for these [=Record types=] are profile-, role-, and capability-specific. An implementation MUST support a core [=Record type=] only where this specification requires that support for a claimed profile, role, or exercised capability.

The capability-bound requirements are defined in [Capability-bound requirements](#section-conformance-requirements).

Additional [=Record types=] MAY be defined by organisations or standards bodies as [=SchemaRecords=] with a subClassOf, referring to the Root Schema of a core Record type or to another Record type.


## Taxonomy ## {#section-types-taxonomy}

```text
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix rw:   <https://w3id.org/recordweb/ontology#> .

rw:InformationRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:Record .

rw:RelationRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:Record .

rw:SystemRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:Record .
```

Every [=Record=] has one primary conceptual purpose. For the purposes of this specification, a [=Record=] is classified as an [=InformationRecord=], a [=RelationRecord=], an [=AgentRecord=] or a [=SystemRecord=].

Any concrete Record Type is defined by a SchemaRecord in accordance with [Record Type Definition](#section-types-definition).

The taxonomy is normative for the interpretation of this specification. It MUST NOT be interpreted as a mandatory implementation inheritance hierarchy or a mandatory JSON type hierarchy. Concrete Record Types are defined by SchemaRecords ([[#chapter-schemarecord]]).

